QB.push(
{i:46,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"Your coordinator spawns a subagent with this prompt: <em>Continue the analysis we discussed and apply the same criteria as before.</em> The subagent returns a generic overview that ignores every criterion the coordinator had established with the user.",
ask:"What is the correct fix?",
a:["Rewrite the delegation to state the criteria explicitly and in full, because the subagent has no access to the coordinator's conversation",
"Give the subagent a tool that reads the coordinator's message history",
"Increase the subagent's context window so it can hold the earlier discussion",
"Instruct the subagent to ask the coordinator for clarification before starting"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The delegation prompt is the subagent's entire world; anything referential resolves to nothing.",
"An over-engineered workaround for writing a complete prompt, and it lets the subagent pull in irrelevant history that pollutes its context.",
"Window size is irrelevant &mdash; the earlier discussion was never sent to the subagent in the first place.",
"Adds a round trip to recover information the coordinator already had and could simply have included."],
e:"Subagents launched via the <code>Task</code> tool start with a clean context containing only their system prompt and the prompt you hand them. Phrases like &ldquo;as discussed&rdquo;, &ldquo;the same criteria&rdquo;, &ldquo;those files&rdquo; and &ldquo;continue&rdquo; are dangling references. A well-formed delegation states the goal, the full inputs (paths, URLs, prior findings quoted verbatim), the output contract, and the quality bar. Being verbose in the delegation is cheap; the verbosity lives in the subagent's isolated window, not the coordinator's.",
k:"Delegation prompts must be self-contained: goal + full inputs + output contract + quality criteria. Never write &ldquo;as discussed above&rdquo; to a subagent."},

{i:47,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"Your coordinator agent is defined with <code>allowedTools: [\"Read\", \"Grep\", \"Glob\"]</code>. You add three subagent definitions and instruct the coordinator to delegate to them, but it never does &mdash; it just does the work itself, slowly.",
ask:"What is missing?",
a:["<code>\"Task\"</code> must be present in the coordinator's <code>allowedTools</code> for it to spawn subagents",
"Subagent definitions must be registered in <code>.mcp.json</code> before they can be invoked",
"The coordinator needs <code>tool_choice: \"any\"</code> so it is forced to delegate",
"Each subagent must list the coordinator in its own <code>allowedTools</code>"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Spawning a subagent is a tool call, and the tool is <code>Task</code>. Without it in the allow-list, delegation is impossible.",
"MCP configuration registers MCP servers and their tools. Agent definitions are a separate mechanism.",
"Forcing a tool call does not conjure a tool that is not in the list; the coordinator would just be forced to use Read, Grep or Glob.",
"Backwards. Subagents do not invoke the coordinator; the relationship is one-directional."],
e:"This is a concrete configuration fact the exam tests directly. Delegation happens through the <code>Task</code> tool, so a coordinator that cannot call <code>Task</code> cannot delegate no matter what its prompt says. When you see an orchestration question where the coordinator &ldquo;does everything itself&rdquo;, check the tool allow-list before you start rewriting prompts. The mirror-image rule also applies: subagents should <em>not</em> normally have <code>Task</code>, or they will spawn their own children and you lose the hub-and-spoke trace.",
k:"Coordinator needs <code>\"Task\"</code> in <code>allowedTools</code> to spawn subagents. Subagents normally should not have it &mdash; that is what keeps the topology flat."},

{i:48,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"You are writing an <code>AgentDefinition</code> for a subagent that must summarise financial filings and must never be able to modify files or run shell commands.",
ask:"Which part of the definition enforces that constraint?",
a:["The <code>tools</code> / <code>allowedTools</code> list, restricted to read-only tools",
"The <code>description</code> field, stating that the agent is read-only",
"The <code>prompt</code> field, instructing the agent never to write files",
"The <code>model</code> field, set to a model without write capability"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Capability is determined by which tools the agent can call; omit Write, Edit and Bash and the actions are structurally impossible.",
"The description drives <em>selection</em> by the coordinator, not permission. It has no enforcement power.",
"An instruction is guidance. It raises compliance probability and cannot guarantee it &mdash; the recurring prompt-versus-code distinction.",
"Models do not have per-capability variants; capability comes from the tools you expose."],
e:"An <code>AgentDefinition</code> has distinct fields with distinct jobs, and the exam probes whether you can keep them straight. <code>description</code> = when the coordinator should pick this agent (a selection signal). <code>prompt</code> / system prompt = how it should approach its work (behavioural guidance). <code>tools</code> or <code>allowedTools</code> = what it is physically able to do (enforcement). <code>model</code> = capability and cost trade-off. Security constraints belong in the tool list, every time.",
k:"AgentDefinition: <code>description</code> drives selection &middot; <code>prompt</code> drives behaviour &middot; <code>tools</code> drives capability. Security lives in <code>tools</code>."},

{i:49,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"DPT",diff:2,
q:"An engineer asks Claude Code to map an unfamiliar 300,000-line monorepo. Discovery generates enormous output &mdash; directory listings, grep hits, file headers &mdash; and by the time the map is built there is little context left for the actual task.",
ask:"Which approach best preserves the main session's context?",
a:["Delegate the discovery sweep to a subagent and have it return only a structured summary of the architecture",
"Raise the model's context window and continue exploring in the main session",
"Use <code>/compact</code> after every ten tool calls during discovery",
"Read files in smaller chunks so each individual tool result is shorter"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The verbose intermediate output lives and dies in the subagent's isolated window; only the distilled result crosses back.",
"Delays the problem. Discovery output scales with the repository, and you have not changed the access pattern.",
"Compaction is lossy and reactive &mdash; it summarises after the damage, and numeric details and specific file paths are exactly what summarisation degrades.",
"Helps at the margin but does not change the total volume of discovery output, which is the actual problem."],
e:"Context isolation is the primary reason to use subagents for exploration, and Claude Code ships an Explore subagent for exactly this. The pattern: the main session states the question, the subagent runs the expensive sweep, and only a compact structured answer (entry points, module boundaries, key files with paths, data flow) returns. Compare the alternatives &mdash; a bigger window is a scaling delay, and <code>/compact</code> is a mid-session rescue, not an architecture.",
k:"Delegate verbose discovery to a subagent so only the summary enters the main context. Bigger windows and <code>/compact</code> are mitigations, not designs."},

{i:50,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"MAR",diff:3,
q:"A coordinator needs to pass a previous subagent's 3,000-word output into the next subagent's prompt, together with instructions on what to do with it.",
ask:"What is the best way to structure that prompt?",
a:["Use explicit delimiters or tags to separate the inherited data from the new instructions, and label what the data is",
"Paraphrase the previous output down to two sentences to save tokens",
"Put the instructions at the very end so they are read last, with no delimiters",
"Send the previous output as a separate first message and the instructions as a second message"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Clear structural separation prevents the model from reading instructions as data or data as instructions, and labelling tells it what it is looking at.",
"Aggressive paraphrasing destroys precisely the figures, names and source references the next agent needs. Trim verbosity by field, not by compression.",
"Position helps, but without delimiters a 3,000-word blob and an instruction paragraph blur together &mdash; and instructions embedded in inherited data are a genuine confusion risk.",
"Not wrong in spirit, but a subagent spawn takes a single prompt; and it still leaves the data unlabelled and undelimited."],
e:"When passing inherited context, use structured formats to separate data from metadata and instructions &mdash; XML-style tags such as <code>&lt;prior_findings&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;task&gt;</code> are the conventional choice with Claude and are unusually effective. Two supporting details: place the bulk data early and the instruction late, since models attend most reliably to the start and end of a long input; and preserve the structure of the inherited data (findings with sources and dates) rather than flattening it into prose.",
k:"Delimit and label inherited context (<code>&lt;prior_findings&gt;</code> / <code>&lt;task&gt;</code>). Bulk data early, instruction late. Trim by field, never by paraphrase."},

{i:51,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"You want three subagents to investigate three independent hypotheses about a production incident, then compare their conclusions.",
ask:"Which spawning pattern is correct?",
a:["Emit three <code>Task</code> calls in a single coordinator turn, each with a self-contained prompt for its hypothesis",
"Spawn one subagent, wait, then spawn the next based on what the first found",
"Spawn one subagent and ask it to consider all three hypotheses in sequence",
"Use <code>fork_session</code> three times from the coordinator's session"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Independent work spawned in one turn runs concurrently, and each prompt carries its own hypothesis and evidence.",
"Correct only if the hypotheses are dependent. Here they are explicitly independent, so serialising triples the latency for nothing.",
"One agent evaluating three hypotheses in one context lets each analysis anchor on the others &mdash; the contamination that separate contexts avoid.",
"Forking is for branching an <em>existing</em> line of work from shared accumulated context, not for fanning out fresh independent investigations."],
e:"Two mechanisms, two purposes. <code>Task</code> spawns a fresh subagent with an isolated context and a self-contained prompt &mdash; use it to fan out independent work, and put several calls in one turn to get concurrency. <code>fork_session</code> branches an existing session so several lines of enquiry share the expensive context you already built &mdash; use it to compare approaches without paying twice to establish the setup. Independent hypotheses with no shared investigative context are a <code>Task</code> fan-out.",
k:"<code>Task</code> fan-out = independent work, fresh isolated contexts. <code>fork_session</code> = branch shared accumulated context to compare alternatives."},

{i:52,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"Your subagents return whatever shape of answer they feel like &mdash; sometimes prose, sometimes bullets, sometimes JSON. The coordinator's aggregation logic breaks constantly.",
ask:"Which fix is most durable?",
a:["Specify an explicit output contract in each delegation prompt, and validate the returned shape before aggregating",
"Have the coordinator use a language model call to normalise whatever shape arrives",
"Reduce the number of subagents so there are fewer shapes to handle",
"Ask subagents to always answer in a single paragraph"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. State the required shape at delegation time and verify it on return &mdash; specify, then trust but check.",
"An extra model call papering over an unspecified interface. It costs latency and tokens and introduces its own error rate.",
"Fewer agents means fewer shapes but the same underspecified contract; it reappears the moment you add one back.",
"Single-paragraph prose is the <em>least</em> parseable option and destroys the claim-to-source structure aggregation depends on."],
e:"Treat the subagent boundary like any other API boundary: it needs a contract. State the exact fields expected, whether they are required, and what to do when information is unavailable (return <code>null</code> or an explicit <code>\"not_found\"</code> rather than inventing a value). Where the shape really matters, have the subagent produce it through a <code>tool_use</code> call with a JSON Schema, which eliminates malformed output structurally rather than hoping for compliance.",
k:"Define an output contract per subagent and validate on return. For hard guarantees use <code>tool_use</code> + JSON Schema, not a formatting request."},

{i:53,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"CGC",diff:3,
q:"You are 40 minutes into a Claude Code session that has built up detailed knowledge of a payments module. You now want to try two different migration strategies and compare them, without either attempt biasing the other.",
ask:"What should you do?",
a:["Fork the session twice so each branch starts from the same accumulated context but proceeds independently",
"Run strategy A, then ask Claude to forget it and run strategy B in the same session",
"Start two brand-new sessions and re-explore the payments module in each",
"Ask Claude to describe both strategies side by side in one response"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Forking preserves the expensive shared context while guaranteeing the branches cannot contaminate each other.",
"Models do not reliably forget on instruction. Strategy A's reasoning anchors the evaluation of B, which is the exact bias you are trying to avoid.",
"Correct outcome, wasteful path &mdash; it throws away 40 minutes of context twice, and the two rebuilds may differ subtly.",
"Useful for a quick comparison, but both analyses share one context and influence each other, and neither gets a real implementation attempt."],
e:"<code>fork_session</code> exists for precisely this shape of problem: an expensive shared prefix plus divergent exploration. Contrast it with the other two session tools. <code>--resume &lt;session-name&gt;</code> continues a named session and is right when the accumulated context is still accurate. Starting <strong>fresh with a structured summary</strong> is right when the context has gone stale &mdash; notably after files changed on disk, where resuming with stale tool results is often less reliable than a clean session seeded with an accurate summary.",
k:"Fork when context is expensive and branches must not contaminate. Resume when context is still valid. Restart with a summary when it has gone stale."},

{i:54,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"CGC",diff:3,
q:"You resume a named Claude Code session from yesterday with <code>--resume refactor-auth</code>. Overnight, a colleague merged a large refactor that moved and renamed several of the files the session had read. Claude begins editing based on the old structure.",
ask:"What is the best course of action?",
a:["Start a fresh session seeded with a structured summary of the goal and current state, or explicitly tell the resumed session which files changed",
"Resume again &mdash; the session will re-read files automatically on the next turn",
"Ask Claude to re-read every file it touched yesterday before continuing",
"Continue and rely on Edit failing loudly when the text no longer matches"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Stale cached tool results are the hazard; either replace the context or explicitly invalidate the parts that changed.",
"Resuming restores the stored context as-is. Nothing re-reads the filesystem on your behalf.",
"Workable but incomplete &mdash; files that were <em>renamed or deleted</em> will not be found, and the session still carries stale reasoning built on the old structure.",
"Edit failing is a late, partial signal: a match can still succeed in the wrong file, and by then Claude has been reasoning from a false model for several turns."],
e:"The blueprint calls this out explicitly: when resuming, the agent must be informed about file changes, and a new session with a structured summary is sometimes <em>more</em> reliable than resuming with stale results. Session context is a cache of what the world looked like when you read it, and caches go stale. The decision rule is simple &mdash; if the world moved underneath the session, prefer a clean start with an accurate summary of goal, decisions made and current state, over resuming and hoping.",
k:"Resumed sessions carry stale tool results. If the filesystem changed, either invalidate explicitly or restart fresh with a structured summary."},

{i:55,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"A subagent finishes its task and returns 60 lines of findings. The coordinator then spawns the next subagent, passing only the phrase &ldquo;build on the previous findings&rdquo;.",
ask:"What will happen and why?",
a:["The second subagent will produce generic work, because it receives none of the previous findings &mdash; only the sentence referring to them",
"The second subagent will automatically receive the previous subagent's output as part of its context",
"The coordinator will error, because a <code>Task</code> prompt cannot reference prior agents",
"The second subagent will call back to the first subagent to retrieve the findings"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Subagents inherit nothing; a reference to context that was never transmitted resolves to nothing.",
"This is the single most common misconception the exam tests. There is no automatic inheritance.",
"No error occurs. The prompt is valid text; it is simply uninformative.",
"Subagents have no channel to each other &mdash; and creating one would break hub-and-spoke."],
e:"Say it once more because the exam asks it in several disguises: <strong>subagents do not inherit the parent's context</strong>. The coordinator must include the previous agent's full output in the next delegation prompt, ideally inside labelled delimiters, preserving structure such as claim-to-source mappings. The cost of including it is real but bounded, and it is paid in the subagent's isolated window &mdash; not in the coordinator's.",
k:"No automatic inheritance between agents. Include prior outputs in full, delimited and labelled, in the delegation prompt."},

{i:56,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"You need a subagent to research a topic and you care most about coverage breadth. Your current prompt says: <em>Search for information about renewable energy storage and report what you find.</em>",
ask:"Which revision most improves coverage?",
a:["State the coverage goal and success criteria &mdash; which sub-domains must be addressed, how many independent sources per claim, what to do if a sub-domain yields nothing",
"Increase the subagent's iteration limit so it can run more searches",
"Add the instruction &ldquo;be thorough and comprehensive&rdquo;",
"Switch the subagent to a larger model"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Explicit criteria convert an open-ended instruction into a checkable target the agent can work against and self-verify.",
"More iterations of an unfocused search produce more of the same shallow coverage.",
"The canonical vague instruction. The exam consistently contrasts it with explicit, checkable criteria.",
"A better model helps at the margin but will still optimise against a vague target."],
e:"Explicit criteria beat vague exhortations &mdash; a Domain 4 principle that shows up constantly in Domain 1 orchestration questions too. For a research subagent that means naming the sub-domains to cover (or requiring it to enumerate them first), setting a source-quality bar, requiring dates and attribution, and specifying what a null result looks like so an empty sub-domain is reported rather than silently dropped. &ldquo;Be comprehensive&rdquo; gives the agent nothing to check itself against.",
k:"Replace &ldquo;be thorough&rdquo; with checkable criteria: which sub-domains, how many sources, what attribution, what to do on a null result."},

{i:57,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"Your coordinator spawns six subagents in one turn. Two fail, one times out, and three succeed. The coordinator's aggregation code assumes all six returned and throws, losing the three good results.",
ask:"What is the design flaw?",
a:["The coordinator has no defined partial-success behaviour, so any single failure destroys work that did succeed",
"Six is too many subagents to spawn in one turn",
"Failed subagents should have been retried automatically before aggregation",
"The subagents should have written their results to shared storage instead of returning them"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Fan-out requires an explicit answer to &ldquo;what do we do when only some come back?&rdquo;",
"There is no magic number. The problem is the all-or-nothing assumption, which would break with two subagents just as surely.",
"Retrying is one possible recovery, but it is a policy choice that depends on error category &mdash; and it still does not define what happens when a retry also fails.",
"Shared storage changes the transport, not the failure semantics. The coordinator still has to decide what to do with three of six."],
e:"Any fan-out needs a partial-success policy decided in advance: which results are on the critical path and which are optional; what the minimum viable set is; whether to retry (and only for transient errors); and how the gap is represented in the output. Combine this with structured error propagation so the coordinator knows <em>why</em> each one failed, and with coverage annotation so the final artefact says which sections are complete and which are partial. Aggregation should be written to tolerate holes by default.",
k:"Fan-out needs a partial-success policy: critical vs optional, minimum viable set, retry rules by error category, and coverage annotation for gaps."},

{i:58,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"DPT",diff:2,
q:"You want a subagent to answer one narrow question &mdash; &ldquo;which module owns retry logic for outbound webhooks?&rdquo; &mdash; while the main session continues coordinating a larger refactor.",
ask:"What should the subagent return?",
a:["A short, direct answer with the specific file paths and symbol names that support it",
"The complete transcript of every search and file read it performed",
"A general overview of the codebase's architecture",
"A recommendation for how the retry logic should be redesigned"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A narrow question deserves a narrow, evidence-bearing answer &mdash; the conclusion plus the specific pointers to verify it.",
"Dumping the transcript into the parent context defeats the entire purpose of delegating for context isolation.",
"Scope creep. It was not asked for, and it costs parent context that the refactor needs.",
"Also scope creep, and worse: it is an opinion formed without the refactor context the main session holds."],
e:"The return payload defines whether delegation actually helped. Scope it deliberately: the answer, the minimum evidence needed to trust it (file paths, symbol names, line references), and an explicit note if the answer is uncertain or the search was inconclusive. Everything else &mdash; the greps, the dead ends, the files that turned out to be irrelevant &mdash; should die with the subagent's context. Ask a narrow question, demand a narrow answer.",
k:"Scope the subagent's return payload as tightly as its question: answer + minimal supporting evidence + an explicit uncertainty note."},

{i:59,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"A subagent's system prompt says it is a &ldquo;senior security engineer&rdquo;. Its <code>allowedTools</code> includes <code>Bash</code>. During a review it runs <code>curl</code> against an internal service to test an endpoint it found.",
ask:"What is the most accurate assessment?",
a:["The tool grant was too broad: <code>Bash</code> confers arbitrary command execution, and the persona prompt does nothing to constrain it",
"The subagent violated its system prompt and needs stronger instructions",
"This is correct behaviour for a security engineer persona and needs no change",
"The subagent should have asked the coordinator for permission first"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. <code>Bash</code> is effectively unlimited capability; if you did not want network calls, the constraint had to be in the tool layer.",
"The prompt never forbade it. And even if it had, the exam's recurring point is that a prompt cannot fence off a capability the tool grants.",
"Persona framing is not authorisation. Unsanctioned traffic to internal services is a real security event regardless of the agent's stated role.",
"Asking permission is a prompt-level behaviour with no enforcement; a hook or a narrower tool grant is what actually gates it."],
e:"<code>Bash</code> is the broadest possible tool grant &mdash; anything the shell can do, the agent can do. Granting it and then trying to fence it off with persona text inverts the enforcement model. Options that actually work: replace <code>Bash</code> with narrow purpose-built tools (<code>run_tests</code>, <code>lint_file</code>); restrict it with permission rules that allow-list specific commands; or intercept with a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that inspects the command and blocks anything outside policy. Grant the narrowest capability that does the job.",
k:"<code>Bash</code> = arbitrary execution. Constrain it with narrow replacement tools, permission rules, or a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook &mdash; never with persona text."},

{i:60,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"The coordinator must pass a list of 40 source URLs plus per-source notes to a synthesis subagent.",
ask:"Which passing strategy is best?",
a:["Pass them as structured records &mdash; each with URL, title, date, key claims and a supporting quote &mdash; inside a labelled block",
"Pass a comma-separated list of the 40 URLs and let the synthesis agent fetch each one itself",
"Pass a prose paragraph mentioning the most interesting sources",
"Pass only the ten sources the coordinator judged most relevant"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Structure preserves the claim-to-source-to-date binding through synthesis, which is what makes the report checkable.",
"Discards the analysis already performed and forces 40 redundant fetches, multiplying latency, cost and failure surface.",
"Prose loses the bindings between claims, sources and dates &mdash; the exact attribution loss the design must prevent.",
"Silent pre-filtering by the coordinator hides a consequential editorial decision and can drop the sources that support a minority finding."],
e:"Passing structured records rather than prose or bare URLs is what keeps provenance intact. Each record should carry the source identity, its publication date (so a 2021 figure and a 2025 figure read as a trend rather than a contradiction), the specific claims drawn from it, and a quote to support each claim. If the coordinator does need to reduce volume, it should filter on stated, visible criteria rather than silently &mdash; and it should say what it dropped and why.",
k:"Pass structured records (URL, date, claim, quote), not prose or bare URLs. Filtering must be explicit and stated, never silent."},

{i:61,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"An architect wants every subagent to use the same large general-purpose system prompt for consistency, differentiating them only by the task prompt at spawn time.",
ask:"What is the main drawback?",
a:["Subagents lose their specialisation, and a generic system prompt with a broad toolset degrades tool selection and focus",
"A shared system prompt is not technically supported by <code>AgentDefinition</code>",
"Identical system prompts cause the coordinator to be unable to distinguish agents",
"Shared prompts cannot be version-controlled"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The system prompt is where role, method, standards and boundaries live; genericising it discards the benefit of having specialists at all.",
"It is entirely supported &mdash; that is why it is a tempting design.",
"Close, but the coordinator selects on the <code>description</code> field, not the system prompt. Identical descriptions would cause that problem.",
"Prompts are text and version-control fine either way."],
e:"Specialisation is the point of a multi-agent system. A subagent's system prompt should encode its role, its method, its quality standards and what is explicitly out of scope &mdash; a security reviewer looks for different things, in a different order, with different severity thresholds than a test-coverage reviewer. Combine a focused system prompt with a scoped toolset and a sharp <code>description</code>, and you get an agent the coordinator can select confidently and that performs its narrow job well.",
k:"Specialise three things per subagent: <code>description</code> (selection), system prompt (method and standards), tool list (capability)."},

{i:62,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"CGC",diff:2,
q:"A long Claude Code session has drifted: answers have become vague, and Claude now refers to &ldquo;typical patterns in this kind of codebase&rdquo; rather than to the specific classes it read earlier.",
ask:"What does this symptom indicate, and what is the best response?",
a:["Context degradation in a long session &mdash; capture key findings to a scratchpad file, then compact or start a fresh session seeded with that summary",
"The model is hallucinating and should be swapped for a larger one",
"The temperature is too high and should be reduced",
"The system prompt has been overwritten and must be re-sent"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Generic, pattern-level answers replacing specific ones is the classic signature of a degraded long-session context.",
"It is not primarily a model-capability problem; the specifics have been crowded out or summarised away.",
"Temperature affects variance, not whether specific facts are still present and salient in the context.",
"System prompts are re-sent on every request by construction; they are not overwritten mid-session."],
e:"Learn this symptom &mdash; the exam describes it precisely. When a long session starts answering from general knowledge instead of from what it read, the specific material has been lost to summarisation or crowded out. The durable remedy is to externalise: write key findings, decisions and file references to a scratchpad file as you go, so they survive any context boundary and can be re-read on demand. Then either <code>/compact</code> or, better, start fresh seeded with that scratchpad.",
k:"Vague, pattern-level answers late in a session = context degradation. Externalise findings to a scratchpad file <em>before</em> you need them."},

{i:63,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"Your coordinator spawns a subagent for a task that will take roughly 15 tool calls. The subagent's <code>allowedTools</code> is empty by oversight.",
ask:"What is the most likely observable outcome?",
a:["The subagent returns an answer generated without any tool access &mdash; plausible-sounding but ungrounded in the actual system",
"The subagent errors immediately with a configuration exception",
"The subagent inherits the coordinator's tools by default",
"The subagent hangs until the iteration cap is reached"],
c:0,
w:["Correct, and this is why it is dangerous: with no tools it simply answers from priors, and the output looks superficially reasonable.",
"An empty tool list is a valid configuration, not an error condition.",
"There is no tool inheritance, just as there is no context inheritance.",
"With nothing to call, the model returns <code>end_turn</code> on its first response. There is no hang."],
e:"Silent capability failures are the nastiest kind, because a language model with no tools does not refuse &mdash; it confabulates. A subagent asked to analyse a file it cannot read will describe what such a file typically contains. Defend with validation at spawn time (assert the agent has the tools its role requires) and with output contracts that demand concrete evidence: file paths, line numbers, quotes, URLs. An answer that cannot cite anything specific is the tell.",
k:"No tools = confident confabulation, not an error. Validate tool grants at spawn and require citable evidence in the output contract."},

{i:64,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"MAR",diff:2,
q:"You are deciding how much context to include when delegating a document-analysis task: the full 12,000-word source document, or a 200-word summary of it.",
ask:"Which is correct and why?",
a:["Pass the full document, because analysis requires the primary text and the subagent's isolated context exists precisely to absorb that volume",
"Pass the 200-word summary, because it minimises token cost",
"Pass the summary first and let the subagent request the full document if needed",
"Pass neither &mdash; give the subagent a tool to locate the document itself"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. You cannot analyse a document you were not given, and absorbing bulk input is exactly what an isolated subagent context is for.",
"Optimises the wrong resource. A summary-of-a-summary is not analysis, and the token saving is trivial against the loss of fidelity.",
"Adds a round trip and relies on the subagent recognising insufficiency &mdash; which, given a plausible-looking summary, it often will not.",
"Sometimes right when the document is huge or its location is genuinely unknown, but here you already have it; making the agent hunt for it adds failure modes for nothing."],
e:"Trimming context is right for <em>verbose tool output</em> where 5 of 40 fields matter. It is wrong for <em>primary source material</em> that the task is fundamentally about. The distinction the exam draws: cut redundancy and irrelevance, never cut the substance of the work. And note the direction of the economics &mdash; passing a large document into an isolated subagent context is the cheap option, because that volume never touches the coordinator's window.",
k:"Trim verbose tool output; never trim the primary material the task is about. Bulk input belongs in the isolated subagent context."},

{i:65,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"After adding a fourth subagent, the coordinator's own context is now dominated by agent definitions, their descriptions and the accumulating results, leaving little room for reasoning.",
ask:"Which change most effectively relieves this?",
a:["Tighten each subagent's return contract and have the coordinator persist accumulated results externally, keeping only the current working set in context",
"Remove one of the four subagents",
"Move the agent definitions into the subagents' own system prompts",
"Increase the coordinator's iteration cap"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The pressure comes from accumulated results, so scope the returns and externalise everything not in immediate use.",
"Solves the symptom by deleting capability, and the pressure returns as soon as the remaining three run longer.",
"Definitions must be visible to the <em>coordinator</em> for it to select agents; moving them out is exactly backwards.",
"Iteration count is unrelated to context pressure &mdash; more iterations would make it worse."],
e:"Coordinator context grows along two axes: fixed overhead (agent definitions, tools, system prompt) and accumulating results. The fixed part is small and necessary; the accumulating part is where the leverage is. Scope each subagent's return payload to conclusions plus evidence, write completed phase results to a durable store or scratchpad, and keep only the current working set live. Structured state persistence has the added benefit of surviving a crash or a compaction.",
k:"Coordinator context pressure comes from accumulated results. Scope return payloads, persist completed phases externally, keep only the working set live."},

{i:66,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"CGC",diff:2,
q:"You want a subagent's verbose output to stay out of your main session, and you want the behaviour to apply automatically every time a particular workflow runs &mdash; not to depend on remembering to delegate manually.",
ask:"Which Claude Code mechanism fits?",
a:["Define it as a skill with <code>context: fork</code> in the <code>SKILL.md</code> frontmatter so it runs in an isolated subagent context",
"Add a line to CLAUDE.md reminding yourself to delegate this work",
"Create a custom slash command that prints the delegation prompt for you to copy",
"Set a global environment variable that forces all tool output to be truncated"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. <code>context: fork</code> is the declarative way to make a skill run in its own isolated context every time it is invoked.",
"A reminder in CLAUDE.md is guidance, and the requirement was explicitly that it not depend on remembering.",
"A command that prints a prompt still leaves the work running in the main context unless it also forks.",
"Blanket truncation would corrupt legitimate tool results everywhere and is not a real setting."],
e:"Skills live in <code>.claude/skills/</code> as a <code>SKILL.md</code> with YAML frontmatter, and the frontmatter is where the operational decisions are declared. <code>context: fork</code> runs the skill in an isolated subagent context so verbose output never pollutes the main session. <code>allowed-tools</code> restricts what the skill may call. <code>argument-hint</code> prompts for required parameters. Putting the isolation in the skill definition makes it automatic and shareable, rather than a discipline each developer has to remember.",
k:"<code>SKILL.md</code> frontmatter: <code>context: fork</code> isolates verbose skills &middot; <code>allowed-tools</code> restricts capability &middot; <code>argument-hint</code> prompts for parameters."},

{i:67,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"Your coordinator passes a subagent the instruction: <em>Use the same output format as last time.</em>",
ask:"Why is this unreliable?",
a:["The subagent has no memory of any previous invocation, so the reference is meaningless and it will improvise a format",
"Output formats are cached per agent definition and expire after one hour",
"Format instructions must be in the system prompt and are ignored in task prompts",
"The subagent will error because the referenced format does not exist"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Each spawn is a fresh context; there is no &ldquo;last time&rdquo; from the subagent's perspective.",
"No such caching exists. Each <code>Task</code> spawn starts clean.",
"Format instructions work perfectly well in a task prompt &mdash; they just have to be stated rather than referenced.",
"No error is raised; the model simply improvises, which is worse because it looks like it worked."],
e:"The same dangling-reference bug as &ldquo;as discussed above&rdquo;, in a different costume. Spell out the required format in the delegation prompt every time, or put a stable format specification in the subagent's system prompt so it holds across all invocations without needing to be restated. Where the format matters structurally &mdash; the coordinator parses it &mdash; enforce it with <code>tool_use</code> and a JSON Schema rather than a formatting instruction.",
k:"A subagent has no history. Never reference &ldquo;last time&rdquo;. Put stable formats in its system prompt; enforce critical shapes with a JSON Schema."},

{i:68,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"MAR",diff:3,
q:"You must decide whether to run five research subtasks as five subagents or as five sequential phases within one agent. The subtasks are independent, each produces substantial intermediate output, and total latency matters.",
ask:"Which factor most strongly favours the subagent design here?",
a:["Independence plus verbose intermediate output means you gain both parallelism and context isolation",
"Subagents always produce higher-quality output than sequential phases",
"Sequential phases cannot use tools, only subagents can",
"Subagent designs use fewer total tokens"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Both criteria that justify delegation are satisfied: the work is parallelisable and the intermediate volume is worth isolating.",
"Not true as a rule. A single agent with continuous context sometimes produces <em>better</em> work on tightly coupled tasks.",
"False. A single agent can use tools across as many phases as you like.",
"The opposite is typical: subagents cost more tokens because context is re-established per agent. You pay that for parallelism and isolation."],
e:"Two criteria justify splitting into subagents: <strong>independence</strong> (so they can run concurrently) and <strong>verbose intermediate output</strong> (so isolation protects the parent context). When both hold, delegate. When neither holds &mdash; tightly coupled steps with small outputs &mdash; sequential phases in one agent are simpler, cheaper and often better, because continuous context lets later steps build naturally on earlier ones. Be able to argue the trade-off in both directions; the exam presents it from both sides.",
k:"Delegate when subtasks are independent <em>and</em> intermediate output is verbose. Otherwise sequential phases in one agent are simpler and cheaper."},

{i:69,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"A subagent returns: <em>I found several relevant items but could not access two of the sources.</em> The coordinator proceeds to synthesis without further action.",
ask:"What is missing from the subagent's response?",
a:["Which sources were inaccessible, why, whether the failure was transient, and what was found despite it &mdash; enough for the coordinator to decide on recovery",
"A confidence score from 1 to 10 for the overall result",
"An apology and a request for the coordinator to retry",
"A recommendation about whether the report should be published"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Structured failure context &mdash; identity, cause, retryability, partial results &mdash; is what makes coordinator recovery possible.",
"Numeric self-rated confidence is explicitly unreliable and is a recurring wrong answer throughout this exam.",
"A vague retry request without the cause means the coordinator would retry blind, which fails identically if the cause was a permission error.",
"Publication decisions are far outside a research subagent's scope and context."],
e:"&ldquo;Could not access two of the sources&rdquo; is the generic-error anti-pattern in prose form. The coordinator cannot tell whether it should retry (a timeout), route around (paywalled), request credentials (permission), or simply annotate a gap (source withdrawn). Structured error context &mdash; failure type, what was attempted, partial results obtained, viable alternatives &mdash; turns that dead end into a decision, and feeds the coverage annotation in the final report.",
k:"Failure reports need: what failed, why, retryable or not, what was salvaged, what alternatives exist. Never a bare &ldquo;could not access&rdquo;."},

{i:70,d:1,t:"1.3",s:"CGC",diff:2,
q:"Your team wants an <code>/explore</code> workflow that any developer can invoke, which always runs in an isolated context, is restricted to read-only tools, and prompts the developer for a target module.",
ask:"How should this be configured?",
a:["A skill in <code>.claude/skills/</code> with frontmatter setting <code>context: fork</code>, an <code>allowed-tools</code> read-only list, and an <code>argument-hint</code>",
"A slash command in <code>~/.claude/commands/</code> containing the exploration instructions",
"An entry in the root CLAUDE.md describing the exploration procedure",
"A shell script in the repository that developers run before starting Claude Code"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. All three requirements map directly onto skill frontmatter fields, and <code>.claude/</code> in the repo makes it shared.",
"<code>~/.claude/commands/</code> is the <em>user</em> location, so it would not be available to the team, and a plain command does not fork context or restrict tools.",
"CLAUDE.md carries instructions and conventions, not invocable, tool-restricted, context-forking definitions.",
"An external script cannot control Claude Code's context isolation or tool permissions."],
e:"Two things to keep straight. First, <strong>location determines sharing</strong>: <code>.claude/</code> in the project repository is version-controlled and reaches the whole team; <code>~/.claude/</code> is personal. Second, <strong>skills carry operational configuration that plain slash commands do not</strong> &mdash; <code>context: fork</code> for isolation, <code>allowed-tools</code> for capability restriction, <code>argument-hint</code> for required parameters. When a requirement mentions isolation or tool restriction, the answer is a skill in the project directory.",
k:"Project <code>.claude/</code> = shared &middot; <code>~/.claude/</code> = personal. Skills add <code>context: fork</code>, <code>allowed-tools</code> and <code>argument-hint</code>; plain commands do not."},

{i:71,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"Policy states that refunds above $500 require manager approval. Your system prompt says so clearly. An audit finds three refunds of $600&ndash;$900 processed autonomously last month.",
ask:"Which change guarantees compliance?",
a:["Intercept <code>process_refund</code> in a hook that blocks any amount over $500 and redirects to the escalation path",
"Strengthen the system prompt with the threshold repeated in several places",
"Add few-shot examples showing large refunds being escalated",
"Have the agent state the amount out loud before calling the tool so it notices the threshold"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A hook is code: it inspects the pending call and refuses deterministically, whatever the model intended.",
"Repetition raises compliance probability. For a financial control, any residual failure rate is an audit finding.",
"Few-shot shapes typical behaviour but cannot bound the tail, which is where the violations live.",
"Chain-of-thought style self-checks help marginally and remain probabilistic &mdash; and they add latency to every refund."],
e:"This is the archetypal Domain 1 enforcement question. Business rules with money, compliance or irreversibility attached must be enforced in code. In the Agent SDK that means a hook intercepting the outgoing tool call &mdash; inspect the arguments, block if the rule is violated, and return a result that redirects the agent toward <code>escalate_to_human</code>. Prompts and examples remain valuable as a second layer, because a well-informed model produces a better conversation around the block, but they are never the control itself.",
k:"Threshold and policy rules &rarr; blocking hook or tool-side precondition. Hooks give deterministic guarantees; prompts give probabilistic compliance."},

{i:72,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"Your agent escalates a complex billing dispute to a human. The human receives only the conversation transcript. Agents complain they spend the first several minutes re-reading everything and often re-ask the customer for information already provided.",
ask:"What should the escalation include instead?",
a:["A structured handoff: customer identifier, issue summary, what the agent already verified and attempted, the reason for escalation, and a recommended action",
"A longer transcript including the agent's internal reasoning",
"A confidence score indicating how difficult the agent found the case",
"An automatic summary generated by a second model call from the transcript"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A structured handoff carries exactly the facts the receiving human needs, in a predictable shape they can scan in seconds.",
"More raw material makes the problem worse &mdash; the complaint is about volume, not scarcity.",
"A difficulty score tells the human nothing actionable about the case itself.",
"Better than a raw transcript, but a free-form summary has no guaranteed fields, is lossy about specifics like amounts and dates, and adds a call and an error source."],
e:"Structured handoff protocols are named explicitly in the blueprint. The receiving human should get: the identifier that lets them pull up the account, a concise statement of the problem, the verified facts (identity confirmed, order number, amounts, dates), what has already been attempted and what the result was, the specific reason for escalation (policy gap, explicit customer request, repeated failure), and a recommended action. Because the fields are fixed, they can be rendered consistently in the agent console and audited later.",
k:"Escalation payload: id &middot; issue &middot; verified facts &middot; what was tried &middot; reason for escalation &middot; recommended action. Fixed fields, not prose."},

{i:73,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"Your agent must complete a three-step workflow: verify identity, check policy eligibility, then act. Logs show the order is usually right but occasionally the agent checks eligibility before verifying identity.",
ask:"When is prompt guidance sufficient, and when do you need programmatic enforcement?",
a:["Prompt guidance is fine when out-of-order execution is merely inefficient; programmatic enforcement is required when it creates a security, financial or compliance risk",
"Programmatic enforcement is always required for any multi-step workflow",
"Prompt guidance is always sufficient if the prompt is specific enough",
"Enforcement is only needed when the steps involve external systems"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Match the enforcement mechanism to the consequence of violation &mdash; that is the judgement the exam is testing.",
"Over-applying enforcement makes systems brittle and removes the adaptability that justifies an agent. Not every ordering matters.",
"No prompt reaches certainty, so this fails precisely for the steps where certainty is required.",
"The location of the system is irrelevant. Consequence, not topology, decides."],
e:"The exam wants nuance here, not a slogan. Ordering that is merely suboptimal &mdash; an extra lookup, a slightly worse conversation &mdash; is fine to guide with prompts, and keeping it soft preserves the agent's ability to adapt to unusual cases. Ordering that gates a consequence &mdash; acting before identity is verified, refunding before eligibility is confirmed &mdash; needs a precondition in code. The question to ask of any ordering rule is: what actually happens if it is violated once in a hundred conversations?",
k:"Enforcement follows consequence. Inefficiency &rarr; prompt. Security, money, compliance, irreversibility &rarr; code precondition or hook."},

{i:74,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"You add a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that blocks a policy-violating call. The agent receives the block, then immediately retries the identical call three more times before giving up and telling the customer &ldquo;something went wrong&rdquo;.",
ask:"What is missing from the hook's design?",
a:["The block result must explain why it was blocked and what the agent should do instead, so it can route to escalation rather than retry",
"The hook should terminate the session on a blocked call",
"The hook should silently allow the call and log a violation for later review",
"The hook should be a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook instead"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A block without an explanation is another generic error &mdash; the agent cannot distinguish it from a transient failure, so it retries.",
"Killing the session strands the customer mid-conversation over a rule that has a perfectly good alternative path.",
"That defeats the purpose entirely: the violation happens, and after-the-fact logging does not undo a refund.",
"<code>PostToolUse</code> runs after execution, which is too late to prevent a policy-violating action."],
e:"Blocking is only half the design; the other half is redirection. The hook's response should say what rule was violated, that the block is not retryable, and what the valid alternative is (&ldquo;refunds over $500 require manager approval &mdash; use <code>escalate_to_human</code> with the dispute details&rdquo;). This mirrors the MCP error-design principle exactly: an error carrying <code>isRetryable: false</code> plus an actionable message produces a graceful path, while a bare failure produces a retry storm and a dead end for the customer.",
k:"A block must carry: which rule, that it is non-retryable, and the valid alternative. Blocks without redirection cause retry storms."},

{i:75,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"Different MCP tools in your system return timestamps in three formats: Unix epoch integers, ISO 8601 strings, and <code>MM/DD/YYYY</code>. The agent frequently miscomputes date differences.",
ask:"Where is the cleanest place to fix this?",
a:["A <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that normalises timestamps to a single format before the results reach the model",
"A system prompt section explaining all three formats and how to convert between them",
"A rule that the agent must call a <code>convert_date</code> tool before comparing any two dates",
"Retraining expectations by adding few-shot examples of correct date arithmetic"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Normalising in a hook makes the inconsistency invisible to the model &mdash; a deterministic fix applied once, centrally.",
"Asks the model to do reliable format detection and arithmetic on every call. It will mostly work and occasionally not.",
"Adds a round trip per comparison and depends on the agent remembering to use it &mdash; probabilistic and slow.",
"Examples cannot make format ambiguity go away; <code>03/04/2026</code> is genuinely ambiguous no matter how many examples you show."],
e:"<code>PostToolUse</code> hooks intercept tool results before the model sees them, which makes them the right place for normalisation, unit conversion, redaction and trimming. The blueprint gives date normalisation as the canonical example. The general principle: do deterministic data transformation in code, and reserve the model's attention for judgement. A useful side benefit is that the same hook can strip a 40-field response down to the 5 fields that matter, addressing context bloat at the same time.",
k:"<code>PostToolUse</code> = transform results before the model sees them: normalise formats, convert units, redact secrets, trim to relevant fields."},

{i:76,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"A workflow requires that <code>process_refund</code> never runs unless <code>get_customer</code> has returned a record with <code>verified: true</code> in the same session.",
ask:"Which implementation is most robust?",
a:["Track verification state server-side and have <code>process_refund</code> itself reject calls when the session has no verified customer",
"Have a hook scan the conversation history for a previous <code>get_customer</code> tool call",
"Instruct the agent to include the verification status as a parameter to <code>process_refund</code>",
"Have the agent state the verified customer ID in its message before calling the tool"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The invariant is enforced by the resource owner using state it controls, so it holds regardless of what the conversation looks like.",
"Better than nothing, but it infers state from a transcript the model influences, and a <em>call</em> to <code>get_customer</code> does not prove it returned <code>verified: true</code>.",
"Lets the model assert its own authorisation. Any model-supplied parameter is untrusted input.",
"Purely conversational and trivially wrong &mdash; the model can state an ID it never verified."],
e:"Never let the model be the source of truth for an authorisation fact. The trust boundary sits at your code: <code>get_customer</code> establishes verified state in a session store your server owns, and <code>process_refund</code> reads that store. Model-supplied parameters are untrusted input in exactly the way user input is untrusted in a web application. A hook is a reasonable additional layer, but the authoritative check belongs where the state actually lives.",
k:"Authorisation state lives server-side, checked by the resource owner. Model-asserted parameters are untrusted input, never proof."},

{i:77,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"A customer writes: &ldquo;My package arrived damaged, I was charged the wrong amount, and I want to change my delivery address for future orders.&rdquo;",
ask:"What is the correct first step in the agent's workflow?",
a:["Decompose the message into three separately tracked items and confirm the full list back to the customer",
"Handle the damaged package first because it is mentioned first",
"Ask the customer which single issue they want to address in this conversation",
"Classify the message into its single dominant intent and route accordingly"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Explicit decomposition plus confirmation makes coverage verifiable and reassures the customer nothing was missed.",
"Order of mention is not priority, and starting on one item with no record of the others is how the other two get dropped.",
"Pushes triage onto the customer and guarantees a second contact &mdash; directly against the first-contact-resolution target.",
"Single-label classification is structurally incapable of representing a genuinely three-part request."],
e:"Decomposing multi-aspect requests into separate tracked items is called out explicitly in Domain 1. The confirmation step matters as much as the decomposition: reading the list back sets expectations, gives the customer a chance to correct a misreading, and creates the checklist the agent verifies against before ending the turn. Note also that the items may interact &mdash; the address change could affect the replacement shipment &mdash; which is a further argument for one conversation rather than three.",
k:"Multi-aspect request: decompose &rarr; confirm the list &rarr; work each item &rarr; verify all resolved or escalated before closing."},

{i:78,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"AAT",diff:3,
q:"An engineer proposes putting every business rule into hooks so that prompts carry no policy at all, arguing that only code is trustworthy.",
ask:"What is the strongest objection?",
a:["An agent that does not know the rules produces poor conversations &mdash; it attempts blocked actions and cannot explain outcomes; prompts and hooks are complementary layers",
"Hooks cannot express business rules involving numeric thresholds",
"Too many hooks will exceed the Agent SDK's hook limit",
"Hooks run after the model responds, so they cannot prevent anything"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Hooks guarantee the outcome; prompts make the agent competent about it. You want both.",
"Numeric thresholds are among the easiest rules to express in a hook.",
"No such limit is a design constraint here, and it is not the substantive objection.",
"<code>PreToolUse</code> hooks run before execution and absolutely can prevent actions."],
e:"The exam's message is <em>enforcement in code, guidance in prompts</em> &mdash; not <em>prompts are useless</em>. An agent that understands the $500 threshold offers the customer the escalation path proactively and explains the wait; an agent that only discovers the rule by being blocked produces a confusing conversation and burns turns on blocked attempts. The layered design is: the prompt teaches the policy so the agent behaves well by default, and the hook guarantees the boundary so the tail case cannot slip through.",
k:"Layer them: prompt so the agent understands the rule, hook so the rule cannot be broken. Enforcement and competence are different goals."},

{i:79,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"CCI",diff:2,
q:"Your CI pipeline requires that Claude Code never pushes commits directly to <code>main</code>, only to feature branches.",
ask:"Where should this constraint live?",
a:["In a permission rule or hook that denies the push command, backed by branch protection on the remote",
"In the CLAUDE.md file as a documented team convention",
"In the prompt passed to Claude Code on each CI run",
"In a code review checklist for humans to verify after the fact"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Deny it locally at the tool boundary and enforce it server-side with branch protection &mdash; defence in depth for an irreversible action.",
"CLAUDE.md is guidance. It shapes behaviour and cannot prevent a push.",
"Same category of protection as CLAUDE.md, applied per run, with the same probabilistic ceiling.",
"Post-hoc review cannot un-push a commit to <code>main</code>, and by then the damage may be deployed."],
e:"Push to a protected branch is irreversible and outward-facing, which puts it firmly in the enforcement category. Two layers: a Claude Code permission rule or <code>PreToolUse</code> hook that denies the matching Bash command, and branch protection on the remote so the constraint holds even if the local configuration is wrong or bypassed. Note the general pattern &mdash; when the risky action is executed by an external system, enforce it in that system too, not only in the agent.",
k:"Irreversible, outward-facing actions get two layers: deny at the agent's tool boundary <em>and</em> enforce in the target system."},

{i:80,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"Your agent correctly escalates when it should, but human agents report that roughly a third of escalations are cases the agent could have resolved &mdash; simple returns within policy that it handed over anyway.",
ask:"Which change best improves calibration?",
a:["Add explicit escalation criteria with few-shot examples showing both what to escalate and what to resolve autonomously",
"Have the agent self-rate its confidence from 1 to 10 and escalate below 7",
"Train a classifier on historical escalation decisions to predict whether to escalate",
"Run sentiment analysis and escalate whenever the customer sounds frustrated"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The root cause is an unclear decision boundary, and explicit criteria with contrastive examples define exactly that boundary.",
"Self-rated confidence is explicitly unreliable &mdash; models are readily confidently wrong, and the scale is uncalibrated.",
"Over-engineering: a training pipeline, labelled data and a maintenance burden to solve a problem that clearer criteria address directly. It also learns the current miscalibration.",
"Sentiment measures mood, not complexity. A cheerful customer can have a genuinely complex policy exception, and a frustrated one can have a trivial return."],
e:"This mirrors a published sample question, and its distractors are a tour of the exam's favourite wrong answers: self-rated confidence, an unnecessary trained classifier, and sentiment as a proxy for difficulty. The correct move is to define the boundary explicitly &mdash; escalate on an explicit request for a human, on a policy gap or exception, or on repeated inability to make progress; resolve autonomously when the case falls inside documented policy &mdash; and to illustrate it with few-shot examples on <em>both</em> sides, since a boundary needs cases from each side to be learnable.",
k:"Miscalibrated escalation &rarr; explicit criteria + few-shot examples of both escalate and resolve. Not confidence scores, not sentiment, not a new classifier."},

{i:81,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"CSA",diff:2,
q:"A customer says: &ldquo;Just let me talk to a person.&rdquo; Your agent responds by asking two diagnostic questions first, hoping to resolve the issue and save an escalation.",
ask:"Is this correct behaviour?",
a:["No &mdash; an explicit request for a human should be honoured immediately, with a structured handoff and no further investigation",
"Yes &mdash; attempting resolution first protects the first-contact-resolution metric",
"Yes, provided the agent asks no more than two questions",
"No &mdash; the agent should first run sentiment analysis to confirm the customer is genuinely frustrated"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. An explicit request for a human is an immediate-escalation trigger; it is not an invitation to negotiate.",
"Optimising a metric against an explicit customer instruction damages trust and usually produces a worse outcome than the metric protects.",
"The number of questions is irrelevant. Any delay after an explicit request is the same error, smaller.",
"Sentiment analysis is both unreliable and beside the point &mdash; the customer stated their request in plain words."],
e:"The blueprint distinguishes <strong>immediate escalation</strong> from <strong>attempt-to-resolve</strong>. Immediate: the customer explicitly asks for a human. Attempt first: the issue is inside the agent's documented scope and no explicit request has been made. The valid escalation triggers are an explicit request for a human, a policy gap or exception the agent has no authority to decide, and demonstrated inability to make progress. Note the asymmetry with the previous question &mdash; that agent escalated too eagerly, this one too reluctantly. The fix in both cases is the same explicit criteria.",
k:"Escalate immediately on an explicit request for a human. Attempt first only when the issue is in scope and no such request was made."},

{i:82,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"You want to guarantee that a compliance-sensitive tool is called with a specific parameter every time, regardless of what the model decides.",
ask:"Which mechanism is appropriate?",
a:["A hook that injects or overrides the parameter before the call executes",
"A tool description explaining that the parameter is mandatory",
"A JSON Schema marking the parameter as <code>required</code>",
"A system prompt instruction stating the parameter's correct value"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Only code sitting between the model's decision and the execution can guarantee the value.",
"Descriptions inform selection and usage; they do not constrain what the model actually sends.",
"Close but insufficient: <code>required</code> guarantees the field is <em>present</em>, not that it holds the correct value. The model can supply a wrong one.",
"An instruction is guidance. It will usually be followed and occasionally not."],
e:"Note the precise distinction the exam draws between C and the correct answer. A JSON Schema with <code>required</code> plus an <code>enum</code> constrains the <em>shape</em> and the permitted value set &mdash; genuinely valuable, and it eliminates malformed calls. But when the correct value is determined by your system rather than by the model (a tenant id, a compliance flag, an audit actor), the value must be injected by code. Schemas constrain structure; hooks control content.",
k:"Schemas guarantee structure (<code>required</code>, <code>enum</code>). Hooks guarantee content. System-determined values are injected in code, never requested from the model."},

{i:83,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"Your agent resolves 55% of contacts against an 80% target. Analysis shows it escalates straightforward returns while attempting complex policy exceptions on its own &mdash; miscalibrated in both directions simultaneously.",
ask:"What is the single most effective intervention?",
a:["Define explicit escalation criteria describing both what must be escalated and what must be resolved, with few-shot examples of each",
"Raise the escalation threshold so fewer cases escalate overall",
"Lower the escalation threshold so more cases escalate overall",
"Add a second agent that reviews every escalation decision before it is executed"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Errors in both directions mean the boundary itself is undefined, so defining it fixes both at once.",
"Moves a one-dimensional threshold. It would reduce over-escalation of simple cases while making the dangerous under-escalation of policy exceptions worse.",
"The mirror image: fewer risky autonomous attempts, but even more simple cases handed to humans.",
"A review agent doubles cost and latency, and it has no better basis for the decision than the first agent &mdash; the criteria are still undefined."],
e:"The diagnostic insight is that errors in <em>both</em> directions rule out a threshold problem. A threshold is one-dimensional: moving it trades one error type for the other. Bidirectional miscalibration means the agent has no usable concept of what makes a case complex, so the fix is to supply one &mdash; explicit criteria plus contrastive few-shot examples on both sides of the line. Notice how this connects to first-contact resolution: over-escalation and under-escalation both depress it, for opposite reasons.",
k:"Errors in both directions = an undefined boundary, not a mis-set threshold. Define the criteria and show examples from both sides."},

{i:84,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"An agent must call three tools in a strict order because each one's output feeds the next. You are choosing between prompting the order and enforcing it in code.",
ask:"What is the deciding consideration?",
a:["Whether an out-of-order execution causes real harm, or merely wastes a call that the agent can recover from",
"Whether the tools belong to the same MCP server",
"Whether the agent uses a large or small model",
"Whether the workflow runs interactively or in a batch"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Consequence of violation is the deciding factor, consistently, across every enforcement question on this exam.",
"Server topology has no bearing on whether an ordering constraint matters.",
"Model capability changes the violation <em>rate</em>, not whether you can tolerate a violation at all.",
"Execution mode affects observability and recovery options, but a harmful violation is harmful in either mode."],
e:"Genuine data dependencies are often self-enforcing: if step two needs step one's output as an argument, the agent physically cannot skip ahead. That case needs no enforcement machinery. The case that does is where skipping is <em>possible</em> and <em>consequential</em> &mdash; the agent can call <code>process_refund</code> with a name instead of a verified id, and the result is a wrong refund. Ask what happens on violation; if the answer is a wasted call, prompt it, and if the answer is a bad outcome, enforce it.",
k:"Ordering questions: ask what a violation actually costs. Wasted call &rarr; prompt. Bad outcome &rarr; precondition in code."},

{i:85,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"SDE",diff:2,
q:"An extraction pipeline must never write a record to the database when a required field failed validation. Currently the agent decides whether the extraction is good enough before calling <code>save_record</code>.",
ask:"What is the correct design?",
a:["Validate against the schema in code before persisting, and reject the write regardless of the agent's judgement",
"Give the agent a detailed rubric in its prompt for judging extraction quality",
"Ask the agent to double-check its extraction before calling <code>save_record</code>",
"Add a confidence field the agent fills in, and only save when it exceeds a threshold"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Data-integrity invariants belong in code at the persistence boundary, where they cannot be reasoned around.",
"A rubric improves the agent's judgement without ever guaranteeing the invariant.",
"Self-checking is a probabilistic improvement on a probabilistic process.",
"Model-reported confidence is uncalibrated and self-assessed &mdash; another recurring wrong answer. It also lets the model authorise its own write."],
e:"Same principle as identity verification before a refund, in a data-quality costume. The agent's job is to produce the best extraction it can; the pipeline's job is to guarantee that nothing invalid is persisted. Validate in code against the schema and the business rules (totals reconcile, dates parse, required fields present), reject on failure, and feed the specific validation errors back to the agent so it can retry with actionable guidance &mdash; which is far more effective than asking it to try harder.",
k:"Validate in code at the persistence boundary. Feed specific validation errors back for a guided retry; never let the model authorise its own write."},

{i:86,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"A hook blocks an action and the agent correctly escalates. But the customer is told only: &ldquo;I'm unable to complete that request.&rdquo; Customers find this frustrating and often re-ask the same question.",
ask:"What is missing?",
a:["The agent should explain the reason and the next step &mdash; what will happen, who will act, and roughly when",
"The agent should retry the blocked action once more before escalating",
"The hook should allow the action so the customer is not disappointed",
"The agent should transfer the customer without saying anything"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A blocked action still deserves a complete customer experience: reason, next step, owner, expectation.",
"Retrying a deterministically blocked action is guaranteed to fail again and adds delay.",
"Disabling the control to improve the experience defeats the purpose of having the control.",
"Silent transfers are the worst outcome &mdash; the customer does not know what happened or what to expect."],
e:"Enforcement changes what the system does; it should not degrade what the customer understands. When a hook blocks an action, the agent should be able to say: this needs manager approval because it exceeds the standard refund limit, I have escalated it with your case details, and someone will contact you within one business day. That requires two things working together &mdash; the block message must be explanatory (not a generic failure), and the prompt must teach the policy so the agent can explain it in its own words.",
k:"A blocked action still needs a complete customer explanation: reason, next step, owner, timeframe. Enforcement should not degrade the experience."},

{i:87,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"You are asked to add an approval step where a human must confirm before an agent executes an irreversible action, without stalling the many reversible actions the agent performs.",
ask:"Which design is appropriate?",
a:["Gate only the irreversible tools behind a human-approval hook, leaving reversible tools to execute freely",
"Require human approval before every tool call for consistency",
"Ask the agent to request approval in conversation when it judges an action to be risky",
"Log all actions and let humans reverse anything problematic afterwards"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Match the friction to the risk: gate the small set of irreversible operations and let the rest flow.",
"Uniform approval destroys the agent's throughput and trains reviewers to click through without reading &mdash; the classic approval-fatigue failure.",
"Puts the risk classification in the model's judgement, so an unrecognised risky action proceeds unapproved.",
"The premise is that these actions are irreversible, so after-the-fact reversal is not available."],
e:"Human-in-the-loop is a targeting problem. Classify tools once, in code, by reversibility and blast radius, then gate the dangerous minority. Approval fatigue is a genuine failure mode: reviewers asked to approve everything stop reading, and the gate becomes theatre. Pair the gate with a good approval payload &mdash; what action, what arguments, what the agent is trying to achieve, what the consequence is &mdash; so the human can decide in seconds.",
k:"Gate on reversibility and blast radius, decided in code. Uniform approval causes approval fatigue and defeats the control."},

{i:88,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"CSA",diff:3,
q:"Your agent's policy document is silent on a specific situation: a customer wants to return a gift purchased 45 days ago, where policy covers 30-day returns and says nothing about gifts.",
ask:"What should the agent do?",
a:["Escalate, because the policy is silent on this case and the agent has no authority to create policy",
"Deny the return, because 45 days exceeds the 30-day window",
"Approve the return, because gifts are not explicitly excluded",
"Ask the customer to provide the original purchaser's account details and re-evaluate"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. A policy gap is a named escalation trigger &mdash; the agent's job is to apply policy, not to invent it.",
"Applies a rule that may not govern this case, and denying a legitimate exception damages the customer relationship.",
"Reads silence as permission, which is equally an invention of policy &mdash; in the more expensive direction.",
"Gathering more facts is fine, but it does not resolve the gap: even with the purchaser's details, no policy covers this case."],
e:"The blueprint lists policy gaps and exceptions among the valid escalation triggers, alongside explicit requests for a human and demonstrated inability to progress. The reasoning is about authority, not difficulty: the agent could produce a confident answer either way, but it has no mandate to set precedent for an uncovered case. Note the shape of both wrong answers &mdash; B and C each resolve the gap by unilaterally interpreting silence, in opposite directions.",
k:"Policy silent or ambiguous &rarr; escalate. Interpreting silence in either direction is inventing policy, which the agent has no authority to do."},

{i:89,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"AAT",diff:2,
q:"A workflow has a step that must run exactly once per case, but retries and reconnections occasionally cause it to run twice.",
ask:"Which combination of measures is most robust?",
a:["Make the operation idempotent with a case-scoped key, and record completion in state the workflow checks before re-running",
"Add a prompt instruction that the step must run only once",
"Detect duplicates after the fact and reverse the second execution",
"Reduce the retry count so duplicates are less likely"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. Idempotency plus an explicit completion record makes the second execution structurally harmless and usually unnecessary.",
"An exactly-once invariant guarded by a probabilistic instruction. It will hold until the conditions that cause duplicates arise.",
"Compensating transactions are a genuine last resort when idempotency is impossible, but they are more complex, slower, and sometimes infeasible for irreversible effects.",
"Lowers the probability without changing the invariant, and reduces resilience to genuine transient failures at the same time."],
e:"Exactly-once is achieved by making at-least-once safe, not by trying to prevent every duplicate delivery. Two mechanisms combine: an idempotency key derived from the case so a repeat call returns the original result rather than performing the work again, and a persisted completion record the workflow consults before dispatching. This is the same reasoning as making a state-changing agent tool idempotent &mdash; assume it will be called twice, and design so that it does not matter.",
k:"Exactly-once = idempotency key + persisted completion state. Do not try to prevent duplicates; make them harmless."},

{i:90,d:1,t:"1.4",s:"CCI",diff:3,
q:"Your CI agent generates code, then reviews its own generated code in the same session and reports it clean. Independent human review later finds several real defects in that same code.",
ask:"What explains the gap, and what is the fix?",
a:["Self-review is weak because the model retains its generation reasoning and is unlikely to challenge its own decisions &mdash; use a separate instance without that context",
"The review prompt lacked sufficient detail and should be lengthened",
"The model needs a larger context window to review its own work properly",
"The review should run at a lower temperature to be more critical"],
c:0,
w:["Correct. The generation context is the problem, so removing it &mdash; a fresh instance seeing only the diff &mdash; is the fix.",
"A better prompt helps a little but cannot dislodge the anchoring that comes from having just argued for these decisions.",
"Context size is not the constraint; the contents of the context are.",
"Temperature governs sampling variance, not critical stance."],
e:"The blueprint states this directly: an instance that generated the code retains its reasoning and is less likely to challenge its own choices, while an independent instance without the generation context finds subtler issues. The pattern is session context isolation &mdash; run review as a separate invocation that sees the diff and the standards, not the conversation that produced it. It pairs naturally with multi-pass review: per-file passes for local defects, then a separate cross-file pass for integration issues that per-file attention misses.",
k:"Never review in the generating session. An independent instance seeing only the diff finds what the author's context hides."}
);
